t Republican lean, Knott's incumbency, and unanimous expert ratings outweigh Barringer's solid fundraising and a national environment tilted toward Democrats; with no polling available, uncertainty stays wide but the favorite is clear.", "watch": "Fresh general-election polling showing Barringer within single digits, or further evidence of Trump drag in North Carolina's red districts, would move this toward Tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Brad Knott (R) is running."]}, "overview": "North Carolina's 13th District, covering Raleigh's southern exurbs and rural counties like Harnett and Lee, leans Republican by about eight points. Brad Knott, first elected in 2024 after the seat was redrawn, seeks a second term. He has out-raised Democrat Paul Barringer, a Sanford-based nominee, roughly $1.3 million to $1.0 million, and holds a cash-on-hand edge. A Libertarian also appears on the ballot. No public general-election polls are available for this race.\n\nRating outlets uniformly favor Knott, with ratings ranging from Likely R to Solid or Safe R, and prediction markets give Barringer about a 15 percent chance. A national environment favoring Democrats and Barringer's solid fundraising keep this short of Safe, but the district's structural Republican lean and Knott's incumbency make him a clear favorite heading into November.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0835, "margin": -8.64}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0743, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1064, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.1381, "margin": -8.0}]}